Basic relationship:
$math{int{a, b, int{g1(x), g2(x), f(x,y) dy} dx}}

Evaluate in 2 Steps:
$math{int{g1(x), g2(x), f(x,y) dy}}
[at constant x -> u(x)]
$math{int{a, b, u(x) dx} = U(b) - U(a)}
U = anti-derivative of u

Presetted values:
D: $math{{y>2x, 0<x<2, 0<y<4}}
$math{int2{D, (x+y)dxdy} = int{0, 4, int{0, y/2, (x+y)dx}dy}}
$math{= int2{0, 2, int{2x, 4, (x+y)dy}dx}}

Basic relationship:
$math{int2{D, f(x, y)dxdy} = int{E, f(x(u, v), y(u, v))|\\d(x, y)/\\d(u, v)|dudv}}
$math{|\\d(x, y)/\\d(u, v)| = det|[[\\dx/\\du, \\dx/\\dv], [\\dy/\\du, \\dy/\\dv]]|}
$math{det|[[\\dx/\\du, \\dx/\\dv, \\dx/\\dw], [\\dy/\\du, \\dy/\\dv, \\dy/\\dw], [\\dz/\\du, \\dz/\\dv, \\dz/\\dw]]|}
$math{(x=x(u, v), y=y(u, v))}

similar to $math{int{f(x)dx} = int{f(g(u))g'(u)du}}
$math{du->0, g(u+du) = g(u) + g'(u)du}

$math{x(r, \t) = r*cos(\t)}
$math{y(r, \t) = r*sin(\t)}
$math{|\\d(x, y)/\\d(u, v)| = |[[cos(\t), -r*sin(\t)], [sin(\t), r*cos(\t)]]| = r}
$math{-> int2{D, f(x, y)dxdy} = int{E, f(x(r, \t), y(r, \t))rdrd\t}}

$math{int{a, b, int{h1(z), h2(z), int{g1(y,z), g2(y,z), f(x,y,z) dx} dy} dz}}

Evaluate in 3 Steps:
$math{int{x_1, x_2, f(x,y,z) dx}}
[at constant y,z -> u(y,z)]
$math{int{y_1, y_2, u(y,z) dy}}
[at constant z -> v(z)]
$math{int{a, b, v(z) dz} = V(b) - V(a)}

For evaluation purposes, double and triple integrals are expressed in iterated forms of interchangeable order

Each iteration is called a partial integral

$math{int{x_1, x_2, int{y_1, y_2, f(x,y) dy} dx}}
$math{= int{x_1, x_2, [ int{y_1, y_2, f(x,y) dy} ] dx}}
$math{= int{y_1, y_2, int{x_1, x_2, f(x,y) dx} dy}}

Rectangular Coordinates
$math{A = int{x_1, x_2, int{y_1=f_1(x), y_2=f_2(x), sqrt(1 + (\\d z/\\d x)^2 + (\\d z/\\d y)^2) dydx}}}

Cylindrical Coordinates
$math{A = int{\t_1, \t_2, int{r_1=f_1(\t), r_2=f_2(\t), sqrt(1 + (\\d z/\\d r)^2 + (1/r^2)*(\\d z/\\d \t)^2) r dr d\t}}}

Presetted values:
Find the area in the first octant cut from the cylindrical surface $math{z = 4 - x^2} by the plane $math{y = x}.

Since boundaries of projection of the required area onto the x-y plane are $math{y = 0}, $math{y = x}, and $math{x = 2}, we have $math{A = int{0, 2, int{0, x, sqrt(1 + (dz/dx)^2 + (dz/dy)^2) dydx}} = int{0, 2, int{0, x, sqrt(1 + 4x^2 + 0) dydx}}}, $math{A = int{0, 2, sqrt(1 + 4x^2) x dx} = (1/12)*(17*sqrt(17) - 1)}

This result can also be found, though with much greater difficulty, by reversing the order of integration:
$math{A = int{0, 2, int{y, 2, sqrt(1 + 4x^2 + 0) dxdy}}}
Thus, ease of evaluation often depends on order of integration

Basic relationship:
Rectangular Coordinates
$math{V = int{x_1, x_2, int{y_1, y_2, f(x,y) dydx}}}

Cylindrical Coordinates
$math{V = int{\t_1, \t_2, int{r_1, r_2, g(r,\t) r dr d\t}}}

Rectangle
$math{V = int{int{int{dxdydz}}}}

Cylindrical
$math{V = int{int{int{\r d\r dz d\t}}}}

Spherical
$math{V = int{int{int{\r^2*sin(\t) d\r d\t d\f}}}}
